00:00:05.720 --> 00:00:06.690
BOSCO ZUBIAGA: Hi.

00:00:06.690 --> 00:00:08.480
My name is Bosco Zubiaga.

00:00:08.480 --> 00:00:11.480
I'm a product marketing manager
for Big Data on the Google

00:00:11.480 --> 00:00:13.280
Cloud Platform team.

00:00:13.280 --> 00:00:15.230
Thank you for joining
our webinar Google

00:00:15.230 --> 00:00:18.800
BigQuery, Google's fully managed
data warehouse for large scale

00:00:18.800 --> 00:00:20.780
analytics.

00:00:20.780 --> 00:00:23.330
I would like to start by giving
you a quick overview of what

00:00:23.330 --> 00:00:24.762
we'll cover today.

00:00:24.762 --> 00:00:27.220
First, I will go through a
quick introduction on the Google

00:00:27.220 --> 00:00:28.790
Cloud Platform.

00:00:28.790 --> 00:00:30.860
Then, I've invited
Chad Jennings and Dan

00:00:30.860 --> 00:00:34.080
Mcclary, product managers
on BigQuery to join me.

00:00:34.080 --> 00:00:37.030
Chad will talk about the
benefits of using BigQuery

00:00:37.030 --> 00:00:39.030
and will show you
an exciting demo.

00:00:39.030 --> 00:00:41.390
Dan will cover the
origins of BigQuery.

00:00:41.390 --> 00:00:43.290
He will then give you
a peek under the hood

00:00:43.290 --> 00:00:44.998
so you have a better
understanding of why

00:00:44.998 --> 00:00:47.690
BigQuery has such a
great price performance.

00:00:47.690 --> 00:00:50.340
Then, he will share with you
some of the latest features

00:00:50.340 --> 00:00:51.500
we've added to BigQuery.

00:00:51.500 --> 00:00:53.030
He will also
explain how BigQuery

00:00:53.030 --> 00:00:55.580
integrates with the larger
Google Cloud Platform.

00:00:55.580 --> 00:00:58.250
And then, we'll pass it back to
Chad to talk about our partner

00:00:58.250 --> 00:00:59.870
ecosystem.

00:00:59.870 --> 00:01:01.250
Let's get started.

00:01:01.250 --> 00:01:02.840
With Google Cloud
Platform, we're

00:01:02.840 --> 00:01:04.879
building a different
kind of cloud.

00:01:04.879 --> 00:01:07.310
The [INAUDIBLE] Google
Cloud of today shares a lot

00:01:07.310 --> 00:01:09.110
of similarities
with private data

00:01:09.110 --> 00:01:12.230
centers and call
facilities of decades past.

00:01:12.230 --> 00:01:15.690
Teams spend a lot of time
thinking about servers, memory,

00:01:15.690 --> 00:01:18.290
network connections,
and storage.

00:01:18.290 --> 00:01:20.870
And while they're not actually
touching physical gear,

00:01:20.870 --> 00:01:22.370
they still have to
manage, monitor,

00:01:22.370 --> 00:01:25.930
and care for virtualized
versions of that same gear.

00:01:25.930 --> 00:01:28.330
We used to use this kind
of cloud inside Google,

00:01:28.330 --> 00:01:29.920
but we realized our
business couldn't

00:01:29.920 --> 00:01:31.750
scale fast enough
unless we reduced

00:01:31.750 --> 00:01:33.940
the complexity of that model.

00:01:33.940 --> 00:01:36.730
We had to reinvent everything
about how we built, deployed,

00:01:36.730 --> 00:01:38.860
and run software
on our Cloud to be

00:01:38.860 --> 00:01:41.760
able to manage that complexity
out of the equation.

00:01:41.760 --> 00:01:45.430
Today, we use 3rd Wave Cloud,
a combination of automated

00:01:45.430 --> 00:01:48.020
services and scalable data.

00:01:48.020 --> 00:01:50.890
And this is what we offer
through Google Cloud Platform.

00:01:50.890 --> 00:01:53.260
Our customers don't
spin up servers,

00:01:53.260 --> 00:01:56.320
but their apps serve millions
of users each minute.

00:01:56.320 --> 00:01:58.900
They don't shard databases,
write failover scripts,

00:01:58.900 --> 00:02:00.289
or patch software stacks.

00:02:00.289 --> 00:02:02.080
And yet, their software
is some of the most

00:02:02.080 --> 00:02:05.090
resilient and
secure ever created.

00:02:05.090 --> 00:02:07.010
This approach lets
our customers code

00:02:07.010 --> 00:02:08.960
without thinking
about infrastructure.

00:02:08.960 --> 00:02:12.140
Consequently, they do a lot
less unproductive infrastructure

00:02:12.140 --> 00:02:13.040
work.

00:02:13.040 --> 00:02:16.610
It's an actual global, elastic,
on-demand set of services.

00:02:16.610 --> 00:02:19.010
And this is how it
translates to big data.

00:02:19.010 --> 00:02:20.930
In a typical big
data world, you have

00:02:20.930 --> 00:02:22.820
a lot of these
different things to do.

00:02:22.820 --> 00:02:24.410
The valuable part
is programming,

00:02:24.410 --> 00:02:28.290
but you also have to provision
resources, manage reliability,

00:02:28.290 --> 00:02:29.690
you have to do upgrades.

00:02:29.690 --> 00:02:32.345
At Google, we take care
of all of that for you.

00:02:32.345 --> 00:02:34.900
You just do the programming,
and the rest of the time

00:02:34.900 --> 00:02:38.730
is spent understanding your
data and finding insights.

00:02:38.730 --> 00:02:41.430
Now, I would like to
turn it over to Chad

00:02:41.430 --> 00:02:43.860
so he can further
explain what BigQuery is

00:02:43.860 --> 00:02:45.600
and how it brings the
core values I just

00:02:45.600 --> 00:02:49.470
explained to data warehouses.

00:02:49.470 --> 00:02:50.220
CHAD JENNINGS: Hi.

00:02:50.220 --> 00:02:51.595
My name is Chad
Jennings, and I'm

00:02:51.595 --> 00:02:54.240
one of the product managers
for Google BigQuery.

00:02:54.240 --> 00:02:55.850
What is BigQuery?

00:02:55.850 --> 00:02:58.800
BigQuery is a fully managed,
serverless, enterprise data

00:02:58.800 --> 00:02:59.594
warehouse.

00:02:59.594 --> 00:03:00.760
What does that mean for you?

00:03:00.760 --> 00:03:04.230
It means no touching servers,
no building indices, no managing

00:03:04.230 --> 00:03:07.620
upgrades or managing
geo-replications.

00:03:07.620 --> 00:03:10.710
When we first built BigQuery
for Google's internal use,

00:03:10.710 --> 00:03:13.470
we underestimated how important
these things really were.

00:03:13.470 --> 00:03:15.990
But after running inside the
company for more than a decade,

00:03:15.990 --> 00:03:19.860
our users really appreciated
this serverless architecture.

00:03:19.860 --> 00:03:23.070
BigQuery operates at
petabyte scale and fast.

00:03:23.070 --> 00:03:25.330
And we'll show you these
demos in just a second.

00:03:25.330 --> 00:03:27.720
We also offer the
convenience of standard SQL,

00:03:27.720 --> 00:03:30.750
so you can port over
your existing workloads.

00:03:30.750 --> 00:03:32.490
Or, I guess and
another benefit, you

00:03:32.490 --> 00:03:35.190
don't have to teach your
analysts a new lingo.

00:03:35.190 --> 00:03:37.510
And lastly, we're durable
and highly available.

00:03:37.510 --> 00:03:40.530
But what do we actually mean
about high availability?

00:03:40.530 --> 00:03:42.600
When you buy a data
warehouse solution,

00:03:42.600 --> 00:03:44.700
you generally just
buy the green boxes.

00:03:44.700 --> 00:03:47.372
These are the actual
servers that you rent.

00:03:47.372 --> 00:03:49.830
You still need to worry about
scaling them, or provisioning

00:03:49.830 --> 00:03:51.600
for peak loads, et cetera.

00:03:51.600 --> 00:03:53.500
When you buy the
solution from Google,

00:03:53.500 --> 00:03:56.730
we take care of this entire
block diagram for you.

00:03:56.730 --> 00:03:59.460
Our idea of highly available
analytics is load your data,

00:03:59.460 --> 00:04:02.310
and then press the
big red button.

00:04:02.310 --> 00:04:04.560
One of BigQuery's
big differentiators

00:04:04.560 --> 00:04:07.646
is its query performance over
extremely large data sets.

00:04:07.646 --> 00:04:09.020
And so, to convey
this, I'm going

00:04:09.020 --> 00:04:12.030
to be showing some queries
over a petabyte of data.

00:04:12.030 --> 00:04:15.720
And it's easy to lose track of
how big that number really is.

00:04:15.720 --> 00:04:17.700
It's a 1 with 15 zeros.

00:04:17.700 --> 00:04:20.190
Or in binary, it's
2 to the 50th.

00:04:20.190 --> 00:04:21.940
But what does that really mean?

00:04:21.940 --> 00:04:24.360
Well, if you took a
petabyte worth of data

00:04:24.360 --> 00:04:27.630
and put it on a stack of 3
and 1/2 inch floppy disks,

00:04:27.630 --> 00:04:29.580
you'd have a stack
three miles high.

00:04:29.580 --> 00:04:32.700
That's 12 times higher than
the Empire State Building.

00:04:32.700 --> 00:04:35.190
If you thought your 4G
connection was pretty quick,

00:04:35.190 --> 00:04:38.220
if you tried to download
a petabyte worth of data,

00:04:38.220 --> 00:04:40.050
it'd take 27 years.

00:04:40.050 --> 00:04:43.380
I really hope those movies
are timeless classics.

00:04:43.380 --> 00:04:46.260
The Library of Congress has
every English language book

00:04:46.260 --> 00:04:48.690
ever written, as well as
significant audio and video

00:04:48.690 --> 00:04:50.220
collections.

00:04:50.220 --> 00:04:53.280
Even that only adds up to
10 terabytes' worth of data.

00:04:53.280 --> 00:04:55.530
You'd need 100 copies of
the Library of Congress

00:04:55.530 --> 00:04:56.801
to add up to a petabyte.

00:04:56.801 --> 00:04:58.800
And I don't know exactly
what this says about us

00:04:58.800 --> 00:05:01.500
as a society, but
the Twitter corpus

00:05:01.500 --> 00:05:04.050
is twice as big as the
Library of Congress.

00:05:04.050 --> 00:05:06.600
So you'd need-- to get
to a petabyte-- you'd

00:05:06.600 --> 00:05:10.350
need 50 times every
tweet ever tweeted.

00:05:10.350 --> 00:05:11.820
Conversely, a
petabyte can actually

00:05:11.820 --> 00:05:13.650
be pretty small, as well.

00:05:13.650 --> 00:05:17.000
Two micrograms of DNA is
a petabyte worth of data.

00:05:17.000 --> 00:05:18.740
And every day--
this is amazing--

00:05:18.740 --> 00:05:21.090
a petabyte worth of
videos is uploaded

00:05:21.090 --> 00:05:23.780
to YouTube every single day.

00:05:23.780 --> 00:05:27.660
And if you had a pretty
significant, but not enormous,

00:05:27.660 --> 00:05:28.350
server farm--

00:05:28.350 --> 00:05:31.860
200 servers logging
50 entries a second--

00:05:31.860 --> 00:05:34.800
you'd have a petabyte worth
of data in three years.

00:05:34.800 --> 00:05:37.680
A petabyte, while being a
pretty vast amount of data,

00:05:37.680 --> 00:05:41.380
it's the size of data that folks
are using every single day.

00:05:41.380 --> 00:05:42.840
And so to show
you how quickly we

00:05:42.840 --> 00:05:44.970
can scan through a
petabyte worth of data,

00:05:44.970 --> 00:05:47.550
we'll cut to a demo right here.

00:05:47.550 --> 00:05:51.720
For this demo, we looked for
a petabyte or bigger data set,

00:05:51.720 --> 00:05:52.950
but we couldn't find one.

00:05:52.950 --> 00:05:54.900
We couldn't even
find one to buy.

00:05:54.900 --> 00:05:56.100
So we built our own.

00:05:56.100 --> 00:05:59.760
We used 40 virtual machines
for two weeks solid to generate

00:05:59.760 --> 00:06:00.900
this data set.

00:06:00.900 --> 00:06:03.330
Let's switch over to the demo.

00:06:03.330 --> 00:06:05.830
The dataset is a
retail data set.

00:06:05.830 --> 00:06:10.340
If we look here,
the Customers table,

00:06:10.340 --> 00:06:14.940
it's an 8-terabyte table
with 4.7 million rows.

00:06:14.940 --> 00:06:20.610
The Products table is a half a
gigabyte with 2 million rows.

00:06:20.610 --> 00:06:24.650
And the fat table is the
Sales Partition table.

00:06:24.650 --> 00:06:30.000
And this one is the big
one, at 1.09 petabytes

00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:32.850
and more than a trillion rows.

00:06:32.850 --> 00:06:35.410
There are some tricks you can
play with columnar data stores.

00:06:35.410 --> 00:06:37.980
And I wanted to show you that
we're not doing any of that.

00:06:37.980 --> 00:06:41.760
We're not taking advantage
of column compression

00:06:41.760 --> 00:06:44.430
by having the same
element multiple times.

00:06:44.430 --> 00:06:46.500
In fact, this table
compresses really poorly

00:06:46.500 --> 00:06:49.320
because it's got so many
unique floating point values.

00:06:49.320 --> 00:06:52.080
And we don't have large
padding fields, i.e.,

00:06:52.080 --> 00:06:55.440
a section of the table that
we're not actually scanning.

00:06:55.440 --> 00:06:57.630
I'll just run through and
show you the preview here.

00:06:57.630 --> 00:07:01.110
You can see that it's actually
populated with unique numbers

00:07:01.110 --> 00:07:03.236
and a lot of them
are floating points.

00:07:03.236 --> 00:07:04.860
The first query that
we're going to run

00:07:04.860 --> 00:07:08.730
is a select star over the
entire table with a filter

00:07:08.730 --> 00:07:09.860
on the Sales Order key.

00:07:09.860 --> 00:07:12.360
And so, what this will do is
it'll pick out all the columns,

00:07:12.360 --> 00:07:13.220
obviously.

00:07:13.220 --> 00:07:15.285
And that's not normal
processing for BigQuery.

00:07:15.285 --> 00:07:17.410
Generally, you just pick
the columns that you want.

00:07:17.410 --> 00:07:20.520
But we wanted to hammer the
database with a big, fat query.

00:07:20.520 --> 00:07:22.590
Let me get this
thing started here.

00:07:22.590 --> 00:07:23.090
OK.

00:07:23.090 --> 00:07:24.600
And we'll move on
to another example

00:07:24.600 --> 00:07:26.100
that's a little bit
more interesting

00:07:26.100 --> 00:07:28.500
while that first query runs.

00:07:28.500 --> 00:07:30.990
The second query here
computes the total sales

00:07:30.990 --> 00:07:33.120
by day for a
particular customer.

00:07:33.120 --> 00:07:36.310
And note that we do
filter by customer,

00:07:36.310 --> 00:07:41.340
because that means we can't just
use pre-computed aggregations.

00:07:41.340 --> 00:07:43.451
I'll go ahead and get
this one running now, too.

00:07:43.451 --> 00:07:43.950
All right.

00:07:43.950 --> 00:07:46.470
So now we're back with the
results from these queries.

00:07:46.470 --> 00:07:51.330
The first query took about
four minutes to process 1.09

00:07:51.330 --> 00:07:54.480
petabytes on a pay-as-you-go
instance that took no time

00:07:54.480 --> 00:07:55.290
to set up.

00:07:55.290 --> 00:07:58.140
That's actually
pretty impressive.

00:07:58.140 --> 00:07:59.580
The second of
these large queries

00:07:59.580 --> 00:08:01.924
took about five and a
half minutes to finish.

00:08:01.924 --> 00:08:03.840
And that's a little bit
longer than the first.

00:08:03.840 --> 00:08:06.480
Because, obviously, we're
not just doing select star.

00:08:06.480 --> 00:08:09.960
We've got the Filter By Customer
key here, as well as doing

00:08:09.960 --> 00:08:13.680
some aggregations, as this query
computes the total sales by day

00:08:13.680 --> 00:08:15.930
for a particular customer.

00:08:15.930 --> 00:08:17.640
But even so, five
and a half minutes

00:08:17.640 --> 00:08:20.100
and four minutes to
scan and do aggregations

00:08:20.100 --> 00:08:23.520
over a full petabyte
table is pretty quick.

00:08:23.520 --> 00:08:25.770
The previous queries
are quite large.

00:08:25.770 --> 00:08:29.370
But we can use partitioning
to save both time and money.

00:08:29.370 --> 00:08:30.930
Let's run essentially
the same query

00:08:30.930 --> 00:08:32.640
but over a period of time.

00:08:32.640 --> 00:08:34.919
Let's say we just want
to find customer sales

00:08:34.919 --> 00:08:36.720
in a month's worth of data.

00:08:36.720 --> 00:08:40.576
We set up a partition here
to select January of 2005.

00:08:40.576 --> 00:08:43.740
And I'll go ahead
and run the query.

00:08:43.740 --> 00:08:46.000
And so, while that's going,
what if we wanted, then,

00:08:46.000 --> 00:08:48.380
to see how fast we could
do-- oop, there we are.

00:08:48.380 --> 00:08:49.470
It finished.

00:08:49.470 --> 00:08:51.935
Less than 10 seconds.

00:08:51.935 --> 00:08:52.560
Now, let's see.

00:08:52.560 --> 00:08:55.200
If we wanted to see actually
how far we can push this.

00:08:55.200 --> 00:08:58.200
So how fast can we
do a partition scan

00:08:58.200 --> 00:09:00.930
on this petabyte sales data?

00:09:00.930 --> 00:09:03.870
I'll just select
January 4th of 2005.

00:09:03.870 --> 00:09:07.121
And let's see how that one goes.

00:09:07.121 --> 00:09:07.620
All right.

00:09:07.620 --> 00:09:10.210
There we go, finished in
less than five seconds.

00:09:10.210 --> 00:09:13.920
That's five seconds to query
an entire petabyte table.

00:09:13.920 --> 00:09:16.606
That's pretty darn quick.

00:09:16.606 --> 00:09:18.480
The other queries that
we ran with partitions

00:09:18.480 --> 00:09:24.660
were a bit small and used simple
scan filter aggregate queries.

00:09:24.660 --> 00:09:25.930
Let me get this one running.

00:09:25.930 --> 00:09:27.450
It's a bit more ambitious.

00:09:27.450 --> 00:09:29.940
And then, we'll
explain what it does.

00:09:29.940 --> 00:09:32.970
This query computes the
seven-day average sales

00:09:32.970 --> 00:09:34.830
across a range of
products defined

00:09:34.830 --> 00:09:36.660
by a regular expression.

00:09:36.660 --> 00:09:39.120
We're also going to join
against the products table

00:09:39.120 --> 00:09:40.670
in order to look up
the product name.

00:09:43.200 --> 00:09:44.040
There we are.

00:09:44.040 --> 00:09:44.910
It's finished.

00:09:44.910 --> 00:09:48.000
Less than 20 seconds.

00:09:48.000 --> 00:09:51.260
In the first two queries, we
scanned the full petabyte table

00:09:51.260 --> 00:09:54.200
and got results back in
just a couple of minutes.

00:09:54.200 --> 00:09:56.570
Then using partitions,
we drastically

00:09:56.570 --> 00:09:58.880
reduced the time and
cost of the queries

00:09:58.880 --> 00:10:01.610
to pull data for
specific time periods.

00:10:01.610 --> 00:10:03.740
The last query is a
relatively complex one

00:10:03.740 --> 00:10:07.220
using regular expressions,
partitions, a join,

00:10:07.220 --> 00:10:10.040
and aggregations to return
the seven-day average sales

00:10:10.040 --> 00:10:13.700
of a range of products taken
from that petabyte table

00:10:13.700 --> 00:10:15.770
all in less than 20 seconds.

00:10:15.770 --> 00:10:17.720
That's pretty darn fast.

00:10:17.720 --> 00:10:19.474
We'll head back to the talk.

00:10:19.474 --> 00:10:21.140
BOSCO ZUBIAGA: Now
that was a cool demo.

00:10:21.140 --> 00:10:22.598
After seeing that,
I'm sure you are

00:10:22.598 --> 00:10:24.970
wondering what makes
BigQuery so fast

00:10:24.970 --> 00:10:27.220
and how BigQuery was developed.

00:10:27.220 --> 00:10:28.850
And now, I'm going
to turn it over

00:10:28.850 --> 00:10:31.940
to Dan so he can explain
where BigQuery came from,

00:10:31.940 --> 00:10:34.536
how it works, and the influence
it has had on the big data

00:10:34.536 --> 00:10:35.035
industry.

00:10:37.704 --> 00:10:38.370
DAN MCCLARY: Hi.

00:10:38.370 --> 00:10:40.650
I'm Dan McClary, part
of the BigQuery team.

00:10:40.650 --> 00:10:43.320
Now, we've heard a lot about
how BigQuery can really

00:10:43.320 --> 00:10:45.690
change organizations for
the better with its ability

00:10:45.690 --> 00:10:47.910
to analyze petabytes of data.

00:10:47.910 --> 00:10:51.102
We've also heard a lot about
what BigQuery practically is.

00:10:51.102 --> 00:10:52.560
But after hearing
all of this, it's

00:10:52.560 --> 00:10:54.000
worth reflecting for a moment.

00:10:54.000 --> 00:10:55.020
How did we get here?

00:10:55.020 --> 00:10:57.100
Where did BigQuery come from?

00:10:57.100 --> 00:10:59.270
And that's what we're
going to look at right now.

00:10:59.270 --> 00:11:02.520
Now, it's important, when we
think about where BigQuery came

00:11:02.520 --> 00:11:05.220
from, to think about Google's
long history of tackling

00:11:05.220 --> 00:11:06.619
big data problems.

00:11:06.619 --> 00:11:08.160
At Google, we like
to publish papers.

00:11:08.160 --> 00:11:10.451
And we like to tell the world
what we've been building.

00:11:10.451 --> 00:11:12.390
Because we think it
helps other organizations

00:11:12.390 --> 00:11:14.430
build things that are
truly revolutionary.

00:11:14.430 --> 00:11:16.890
This goes all the way
back to our earliest days.

00:11:16.890 --> 00:11:21.240
In 2002 and 2004, we published
a pair of papers about systems

00:11:21.240 --> 00:11:23.760
called GFS and MapReduce.

00:11:23.760 --> 00:11:28.370
These papers actually gave the
initial idea to Doug Cutting

00:11:28.370 --> 00:11:30.870
for the Apache Hadoop project,
which has taken the big data

00:11:30.870 --> 00:11:32.310
world by storm.

00:11:32.310 --> 00:11:34.260
More recently, we've
published papers

00:11:34.260 --> 00:11:36.990
on Dataflow and
TensorFlow, systems

00:11:36.990 --> 00:11:39.180
which have generated
a lot of excitement

00:11:39.180 --> 00:11:40.830
around streaming
data and analytics,

00:11:40.830 --> 00:11:42.580
and machine learning.

00:11:42.580 --> 00:11:44.982
One of the papers that we
published was called Dremel.

00:11:44.982 --> 00:11:46.440
And this was about
a system that we

00:11:46.440 --> 00:11:48.330
built inside Google
that's been really

00:11:48.330 --> 00:11:51.300
important to the development
of the big data ecosystem.

00:11:51.300 --> 00:11:52.830
What was the Dremel paper?

00:11:52.830 --> 00:11:55.770
Well, the Dremel paper
was based on a system

00:11:55.770 --> 00:11:58.560
that was written by an engineer
here called Andrey Gubarev.

00:11:58.560 --> 00:11:59.886
Now, Andrey had a problem.

00:11:59.886 --> 00:12:01.260
He was trying to
write MapReduces

00:12:01.260 --> 00:12:03.130
over many, many, many logs.

00:12:03.130 --> 00:12:05.190
And it took a long time.

00:12:05.190 --> 00:12:07.510
This is a problem analogous
to what many people

00:12:07.510 --> 00:12:10.710
using open source big data
problems have dealt with.

00:12:10.710 --> 00:12:13.050
Now, what Andrey did
is he started storing

00:12:13.050 --> 00:12:15.390
his logs in a columnar format.

00:12:15.390 --> 00:12:17.820
And then, he wrote a little
SQL query engine over it.

00:12:17.820 --> 00:12:19.320
And it was way faster.

00:12:19.320 --> 00:12:20.880
And that was really interesting.

00:12:20.880 --> 00:12:23.190
And soon he'd attracted
the attention of an intern

00:12:23.190 --> 00:12:24.690
and some other engineers.

00:12:24.690 --> 00:12:26.160
And there was a snowball effect.

00:12:26.160 --> 00:12:28.300
And Dremel became a big deal.

00:12:28.300 --> 00:12:30.930
And this paper was a bit of
a hallmark for the big data

00:12:30.930 --> 00:12:31.920
space, as well.

00:12:31.920 --> 00:12:35.400
Because this paper gave rise to
both the Apache Parquet Storage

00:12:35.400 --> 00:12:38.490
project and to SQL and Hadoop
solutions like Cloudera's

00:12:38.490 --> 00:12:40.560
Impala and Apache Drill.

00:12:40.560 --> 00:12:42.790
Now, where is Dremel today?

00:12:42.790 --> 00:12:44.820
Well, Dremel today is
still used inside Google.

00:12:44.820 --> 00:12:48.060
Dremel is Google's mission
critical data warehouse.

00:12:48.060 --> 00:12:49.755
It's been in production
for 10 years.

00:12:49.755 --> 00:12:52.130
It's in every
Google data center.

00:12:52.130 --> 00:12:55.350
And for internal usage, we
query on the order exabytes

00:12:55.350 --> 00:12:56.490
of data a month.

00:12:56.490 --> 00:12:58.710
That's an order of magnitude
bigger than the petabyte

00:12:58.710 --> 00:13:00.490
we were talking about earlier.

00:13:00.490 --> 00:13:01.910
And what does that stack up to?

00:13:01.910 --> 00:13:06.210
That sets up to quadrillions
of rows scanned every month.

00:13:06.210 --> 00:13:08.580
And 80% of Googlers
use it every day.

00:13:08.580 --> 00:13:11.210
Some of them don't even know it.

00:13:11.210 --> 00:13:13.430
And Dremel is BigQuery.

00:13:13.430 --> 00:13:16.400
When you use Google Cloud
BigQuery, you're using Dremel.

00:13:16.400 --> 00:13:17.570
It's the same system.

00:13:17.570 --> 00:13:20.480
We launched it to the
public as a beta in 2010

00:13:20.480 --> 00:13:22.880
and made it generally
available in 2012.

00:13:22.880 --> 00:13:25.760
It's the same engineering
team and the same codebase.

00:13:25.760 --> 00:13:29.720
It's exactly the same
system brought to you.

00:13:29.720 --> 00:13:32.630
In the past five years, we've
been busy improving Dremel.

00:13:32.630 --> 00:13:34.850
We just didn't write
it and let it sit idle.

00:13:34.850 --> 00:13:36.350
You take a look
at the graph here,

00:13:36.350 --> 00:13:37.850
the yellow line
shows you the number

00:13:37.850 --> 00:13:40.850
of code changes to Dremel
over the last five years.

00:13:40.850 --> 00:13:43.940
And these are the same code
changes that go into BigQuery.

00:13:43.940 --> 00:13:47.480
Each of the blue flags
represents a major milestone

00:13:47.480 --> 00:13:49.280
that we think is
important to customers.

00:13:49.280 --> 00:13:51.530
These are things like Dremel
X, which we'll talk about

00:13:51.530 --> 00:13:53.238
in a moment, and some
of the other things

00:13:53.238 --> 00:13:56.150
that we've done around faster
data ingestion and better

00:13:56.150 --> 00:13:57.740
support for the SQL language.

00:13:57.740 --> 00:13:59.540
Well, that's five
years of innovation.

00:13:59.540 --> 00:14:01.137
But what have we done lately?

00:14:01.137 --> 00:14:02.720
Well, to take a look
at this, we first

00:14:02.720 --> 00:14:05.510
have to take a look at
BigQuery's architecture.

00:14:05.510 --> 00:14:08.570
Now, importantly,
BigQuery disaggregates

00:14:08.570 --> 00:14:10.090
Storage and Compute.

00:14:10.090 --> 00:14:12.350
This allows us some
real advantages.

00:14:12.350 --> 00:14:14.510
We can summon thousands
of virtual CPUs

00:14:14.510 --> 00:14:17.050
in an instant to
service a query.

00:14:17.050 --> 00:14:20.570
However, this is all dependent
on Google's incredibly fast

00:14:20.570 --> 00:14:21.950
data center networking.

00:14:21.950 --> 00:14:24.470
Because our networks
have a petabit bisection,

00:14:24.470 --> 00:14:26.600
it means that even
though that Storage is

00:14:26.600 --> 00:14:29.750
separate from Compute,
it runs as if it was all

00:14:29.750 --> 00:14:32.360
on the same gigantic
machine, so that petabyte

00:14:32.360 --> 00:14:35.690
can be scanned and processed
incredibly quickly.

00:14:35.690 --> 00:14:37.670
One of the improvements
we made was to the way

00:14:37.670 --> 00:14:40.400
that Dremel actually
allocates these virtual CPUs

00:14:40.400 --> 00:14:42.300
to the task at hand.

00:14:42.300 --> 00:14:46.070
Let's take a look at the Dremel
architecture from 2006 to 2015.

00:14:46.070 --> 00:14:48.920
In this case, we relied on what
we called a Long-lived Serving

00:14:48.920 --> 00:14:50.162
Tree.

00:14:50.162 --> 00:14:52.370
This looks very similar--
to those of you coming from

00:14:52.370 --> 00:14:53.570
the database world--

00:14:53.570 --> 00:14:55.730
to a directed, acyclic
processing graph,

00:14:55.730 --> 00:14:56.990
or a query plan.

00:14:56.990 --> 00:14:59.180
And that's exactly what it is.

00:14:59.180 --> 00:15:01.010
This was very useful,
in terms of be

00:15:01.010 --> 00:15:03.620
able to optimally scan
and process lots of data

00:15:03.620 --> 00:15:05.570
for common queries.

00:15:05.570 --> 00:15:09.530
However, for certain kinds of
queries, it maybe wasn't ideal.

00:15:09.530 --> 00:15:12.320
Recently, we produced
something called Dremel X.

00:15:12.320 --> 00:15:14.750
And this architecture
is much more dynamic.

00:15:14.750 --> 00:15:16.955
You can think about this
as Dremel's optimizer,

00:15:16.955 --> 00:15:20.690
or BigQuery's optimizer,
getting much smarter

00:15:20.690 --> 00:15:23.810
about how to allocate
compute units in an instant

00:15:23.810 --> 00:15:25.670
to the queries at hand.

00:15:25.670 --> 00:15:26.970
What does this mean for you?

00:15:26.970 --> 00:15:29.300
This means massive
joins get faster.

00:15:29.300 --> 00:15:31.475
Most queries got an order
of magnitude faster,

00:15:31.475 --> 00:15:34.790
and everything just works.

00:15:34.790 --> 00:15:37.690
And Dremel X is available
as part of BigQuery now.

00:15:37.690 --> 00:15:40.540
Users who switch to the system
didn't even know they switched.

00:15:40.540 --> 00:15:43.600
They just woke up and
queries got much faster.

00:15:43.600 --> 00:15:45.130
And we continue to
make improvements

00:15:45.130 --> 00:15:48.370
like this to BigQuery everyday.

00:15:48.370 --> 00:15:49.840
We've talked a
little bit about how

00:15:49.840 --> 00:15:53.500
BigQuery takes advantage
of a legacy of big data

00:15:53.500 --> 00:15:55.450
advances inside Google.

00:15:55.450 --> 00:15:58.660
But where does it fit within
Google Cloud platform's data

00:15:58.660 --> 00:16:01.710
architecture or your
own data architecture?

00:16:01.710 --> 00:16:03.780
Let's take a look.

00:16:03.780 --> 00:16:06.900
If we zoom in, one of the things
we might really care about

00:16:06.900 --> 00:16:09.930
is the fundamentals of data
warehousing or big data

00:16:09.930 --> 00:16:10.800
systems.

00:16:10.800 --> 00:16:12.137
Where do I load and store data?

00:16:12.137 --> 00:16:14.220
And then, how am I going
to analyze and understand

00:16:14.220 --> 00:16:15.660
that data?

00:16:15.660 --> 00:16:18.000
If we look at the Load and
Store side of the house,

00:16:18.000 --> 00:16:19.500
you can see three
things that really

00:16:19.500 --> 00:16:22.590
form the heart of that part
of our reference architecture.

00:16:22.590 --> 00:16:24.360
You have Google
Cloud Dataflow, which

00:16:24.360 --> 00:16:26.700
is used for ETL,
streaming aggregation,

00:16:26.700 --> 00:16:28.140
and a number of other things.

00:16:28.140 --> 00:16:29.640
You have Google
Cloud Storage, which

00:16:29.640 --> 00:16:33.132
is designed for incredibly
low-cost, planet-scale storage.

00:16:33.132 --> 00:16:35.340
And then, we have systems
like Google BigTable, which

00:16:35.340 --> 00:16:39.090
is designed for iOT data, time
series, and other things that

00:16:39.090 --> 00:16:42.750
tend to be column
family in nature.

00:16:42.750 --> 00:16:45.150
On the analysis side,
we've got BigQuery,

00:16:45.150 --> 00:16:47.662
which, obviously, we've been
talking quite a lot about.

00:16:47.662 --> 00:16:49.620
But for those of you who
want to take advantage

00:16:49.620 --> 00:16:53.130
of the open source big data
space like Hadoop and Spark,

00:16:53.130 --> 00:16:54.709
we have Google Cloud Dataproc.

00:16:54.709 --> 00:16:56.250
And this allows you
to run the latest

00:16:56.250 --> 00:16:59.070
versions of Hadoop and Spark
in a fully managed cluster

00:16:59.070 --> 00:17:00.372
in seconds.

00:17:00.372 --> 00:17:02.080
I have an arrow between
these two things.

00:17:02.080 --> 00:17:03.420
It says full integration.

00:17:03.420 --> 00:17:04.780
What does that mean?

00:17:04.780 --> 00:17:06.420
Well, full integration
means that I

00:17:06.420 --> 00:17:09.329
don't have to draw little arrows
between each of these hexagons

00:17:09.329 --> 00:17:11.400
to tell you, this
thing connects to that,

00:17:11.400 --> 00:17:13.109
this other thing
connects this way,

00:17:13.109 --> 00:17:15.119
you can only do this
with this other thing.

00:17:15.119 --> 00:17:17.790
Full integration means all of
these products in Google Cloud

00:17:17.790 --> 00:17:20.940
Platform are designed to
work together, seamlessly,

00:17:20.940 --> 00:17:24.660
without any extra
effort on your part.

00:17:24.660 --> 00:17:26.162
But what about other things?

00:17:26.162 --> 00:17:28.620
There are other things that we
do with data other than load

00:17:28.620 --> 00:17:30.720
and store it, and analyze
and understand it.

00:17:30.720 --> 00:17:32.719
So let's take a bit
of a broader look.

00:17:32.719 --> 00:17:35.010
We want you to be able to
ingest data into Google Cloud

00:17:35.010 --> 00:17:36.345
Platform from anywhere.

00:17:36.345 --> 00:17:38.220
And we do this, in part,
through technologies

00:17:38.220 --> 00:17:40.680
like our own Google
Cloud Pub/Sub, which

00:17:40.680 --> 00:17:42.690
allows you to do
scalable message queuing

00:17:42.690 --> 00:17:45.540
from anywhere in the world, but
also through tools that you may

00:17:45.540 --> 00:17:48.270
use today, like ETL
tools such as Talend,

00:17:48.270 --> 00:17:51.000
or data wrangling
tools like Trifacta.

00:17:51.000 --> 00:17:53.160
And on the other side, we
know that it's important

00:17:53.160 --> 00:17:55.920
that you be able to visualize
data the way you want to,

00:17:55.920 --> 00:17:58.950
explore it easily, and
share it with others, which

00:17:58.950 --> 00:18:02.850
is why both BigQuery and
Google Cloud Dataproc are fully

00:18:02.850 --> 00:18:05.964
integrated with solutions like
Tableau and MicroStrategy,

00:18:05.964 --> 00:18:07.380
as well as with
our own solutions,

00:18:07.380 --> 00:18:11.910
such as Google Analytics 360
and its Data Studio 360 project,

00:18:11.910 --> 00:18:14.310
and with things like Google
Cloud Datalab, which provides

00:18:14.310 --> 00:18:16.560
data scientists a
flexible environment

00:18:16.560 --> 00:18:18.390
to both write their
own code and integrate

00:18:18.390 --> 00:18:21.150
with systems like BigQuery.

00:18:21.150 --> 00:18:23.400
We talked earlier in this
webcast about the fact

00:18:23.400 --> 00:18:25.520
that BigQuery is fully managed.

00:18:25.520 --> 00:18:27.530
And what is fully managed mean?

00:18:27.530 --> 00:18:30.180
Well, we think fully
managed means getting more.

00:18:30.180 --> 00:18:31.800
It means that as
a BigQuery user,

00:18:31.800 --> 00:18:34.980
you get solutions like
Dynamic Execution immediately.

00:18:34.980 --> 00:18:37.920
You wake up one day and
BigQuery just get a lot faster.

00:18:37.920 --> 00:18:39.799
Your queries got
easier to write.

00:18:39.799 --> 00:18:42.090
It also means things like
Project Poseidon come to you.

00:18:42.090 --> 00:18:44.030
And you wake up, and
things get better.

00:18:44.030 --> 00:18:47.350
Poseidon allows you to all of a
sudden load new kinds of files,

00:18:47.350 --> 00:18:49.050
such as AVRO file formats.

00:18:49.050 --> 00:18:52.002
It also means that most
ingestions got 10 times faster.

00:18:52.002 --> 00:18:53.460
And you get this
all, just one day,

00:18:53.460 --> 00:18:55.410
without having to do anything.

00:18:55.410 --> 00:18:58.080
And then, things like consistent
scheduling, as we mentioned,

00:18:58.080 --> 00:18:59.640
BigQuery's
architecture allows us

00:18:59.640 --> 00:19:02.430
to summon a vast number
of CPUs instantaneously

00:19:02.430 --> 00:19:04.230
to service your query.

00:19:04.230 --> 00:19:06.720
But at the same time, we
make sure you're always

00:19:06.720 --> 00:19:08.880
going to have more
than enough resources

00:19:08.880 --> 00:19:10.710
to handle your queries.

00:19:10.710 --> 00:19:13.800
And so, fully managed means
you get the latest innovations

00:19:13.800 --> 00:19:16.080
without having to go
through painful upgrades,

00:19:16.080 --> 00:19:18.300
whether it's on the query
execution side, or the data

00:19:18.300 --> 00:19:19.252
storage side.

00:19:19.252 --> 00:19:21.210
And it also means you
always have the resources

00:19:21.210 --> 00:19:23.024
you need to process your data.

00:19:23.024 --> 00:19:24.690
Now, I'm going to
throw it back to Chad,

00:19:24.690 --> 00:19:27.106
who's going to talk a little
bit more about other partners

00:19:27.106 --> 00:19:30.381
and how they integrate
with Google BigQuery.

00:19:30.381 --> 00:19:31.880
CHAD JENNINGS:
BigQuery isn't trying

00:19:31.880 --> 00:19:34.190
to be everything to everyone.

00:19:34.190 --> 00:19:36.320
In places where we're not
building functionality,

00:19:36.320 --> 00:19:38.810
we need to partner with
outstanding companies

00:19:38.810 --> 00:19:41.780
to provide that functionality
to our customers.

00:19:41.780 --> 00:19:44.000
You can see here four
different functional areas

00:19:44.000 --> 00:19:47.390
from visualization
to data preparation.

00:19:47.390 --> 00:19:50.540
It's important for BigQuery to
use these other technologies

00:19:50.540 --> 00:19:53.300
so that you, as a company,
if you use MicroStrategy,

00:19:53.300 --> 00:19:56.630
or if you use Talend or Tableau,
you can still use those tools,

00:19:56.630 --> 00:19:59.150
but have BigQuery
as your backend.

00:19:59.150 --> 00:20:01.310
Thanks for taking the
time to watch the webinar.

00:20:01.310 --> 00:20:02.990
On behalf of my other
speakers, I just

00:20:02.990 --> 00:20:04.820
wanted to extend that thank you.

00:20:04.820 --> 00:20:06.900
And if you're curious
about learning more,

00:20:06.900 --> 00:20:09.860
you can go to
cloud.google.com/bigquery.

00:20:09.860 --> 00:20:11.060
Take care.

00:20:11.060 --> 00:20:14.710
[MUSIC PLAYING]

